1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,722
Before a computer can improve how a chip is split, it needs a first split, called

2
00:00:05,722 --> 00:00:07,418
the initial bipartition.

3
00:00:07,418 --> 00:00:11,869
You will meet three ways to build one: random, greedy and grow.

4
00:00:11,869 --> 00:00:15,896
On one small graph, they give cuts from three to sixteen.

5
00:00:15,896 --> 00:00:20,064
The habit that fixes it: try several starts, keep the best.

6
00:00:20,064 --> 00:00:25,459
A chip is made of small building blocks called cells, joined by wires.

7
00:00:25,459 --> 00:00:28,620
Our example has five cells, named A to E.

8
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:32,088
Some pairs share many wires, others only one.

9
00:00:32,088 --> 00:00:35,485
We draw each cell as a dot, called a node.

10
00:00:35,485 --> 00:00:38,397
A line between two nodes is an edge.

11
00:00:38,397 --> 00:00:43,089
Its number, the weight, counts the wires between the pair.

12
00:00:43,089 --> 00:00:47,376
A, B and D, E are the heaviest, with five wires each.

13
00:00:47,376 --> 00:00:51,482
Splitting the cells into two sides is called a bipartition.

14
00:00:51,482 --> 00:00:56,006
Edges that cross between the two sides are cut, and drawn in red.

15
00:00:56,006 --> 00:01:00,600
The cutsize adds up their weights: here, two plus one makes three.

16
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:06,018
Putting every cell on one side gives a cut of zero, but that split is useless.

17
00:01:06,018 --> 00:01:09,978
So we also check balance: how many cells each side holds.

18
00:01:09,978 --> 00:01:14,424
A split is legal when no side holds more than three of the five.

19
00:01:14,424 --> 00:01:18,873
Labs four and five teach two famous methods, called KL and FM.

20
00:01:18,873 --> 00:01:23,465
They improve a split by moving cells from one side to the other.

21
00:01:23,465 --> 00:01:26,837
But KL and FM need a legal split to start from.

22
00:01:26,837 --> 00:01:32,720
Think of picking two teams: first any two teams, then swaps to make the game fair.

23
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:36,021
A good first split leaves less swapping to do.

24
00:01:36,021 --> 00:01:41,617
Pause and guess: could a split made at random be as good as this one, with cut

25
00:01:41,617 --> 00:01:42,048
three?

26
00:01:42,048 --> 00:01:47,205
Three short recipes can build a first split: random, greedy and grow.

27
00:01:47,205 --> 00:01:51,689
And yes, a random split can reach cut three, if it is lucky.

28
00:01:51,689 --> 00:01:56,472
Every recipe puts two cells on one side, and three on the other.

29
00:01:56,472 --> 00:01:58,191
Recipe one is random.

30
00:01:58,191 --> 00:02:03,755
Shuffle the five names like cards, and deal two of them to one side.

31
00:02:03,755 --> 00:02:10,466
A number called the seed fixes the shuffle, so the same seed always gives the same

32
00:02:10,466 --> 00:02:10,957
split.

33
00:02:10,957 --> 00:02:13,658
Seed five deals D and E together.

34
00:02:13,658 --> 00:02:16,440
Only C, D and C, E are cut: three.

35
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:22,115
Recipe two is greedy: at each step, it takes what looks best right now.

36
00:02:22,115 --> 00:02:26,112
It keeps the heaviest edge, A, B, inside one side.

37
00:02:26,112 --> 00:02:30,429
D, which has no wire to A or B, starts the other side.

38
00:02:30,429 --> 00:02:34,425
Each other cell joins the side where it cuts less.

39
00:02:34,425 --> 00:02:36,264
The cut is three again.

40
00:02:36,264 --> 00:02:42,771
Recipe three is grow: a side spreads from one start node, like a drop of ink on

41
00:02:42,771 --> 00:02:43,266
paper.

42
00:02:43,266 --> 00:02:44,337
Start from D.

43
00:02:44,337 --> 00:02:48,455
The heaviest edge leaving D goes to E, so E joins.

44
00:02:48,455 --> 00:02:51,750
Two cells are enough, so the side stops.

45
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:55,704
A, B and C form the other side: cut three again.

46
00:02:55,704 --> 00:02:58,025
All three found cut three.

47
00:02:58,025 --> 00:03:00,792
Pause and guess: was that luck?

48
00:03:00,792 --> 00:03:06,246
A single unlucky shuffle can make the cut more than five times worse.

49
00:03:06,246 --> 00:03:08,380
So yes, it was partly luck.

50
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:13,992
With seed three, B and D land on one side, and A, C and E on the other.

51
00:03:13,992 --> 00:03:18,116
Let's add up the cut, edge by edge.

52
00:03:18,116 --> 00:03:23,537
A, B five, B, C four, C, D two, and D, E five.

53
00:03:23,537 --> 00:03:25,776
That makes sixteen.

54
00:03:25,776 --> 00:03:30,528
Both heavy edges are cut, so this is the worst legal split of all.

55
00:03:30,528 --> 00:03:34,488
Over sixteen seeds, the cut goes from three to sixteen.

56
00:03:34,488 --> 00:03:38,088
Only three of those seeds hit the best cut, three.

57
00:03:38,088 --> 00:03:42,658
Grow has no luck in it, but it has a choice: the start node.

58
00:03:42,658 --> 00:03:45,096
Let's start from A instead of D.

59
00:03:45,096 --> 00:03:50,495
The heaviest edge leaving A goes to B, with weight five, so B joins A.

60
00:03:50,495 --> 00:03:54,120
With two cells, the side is full, and it stops.

61
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:59,139
C, D and E form the other side, so A, C and B, C are cut.

62
00:03:59,139 --> 00:04:03,718
One plus four makes five, which is worse than three.

63
00:04:03,718 --> 00:04:07,680
Starting from C is even worse: a cut of nine.

64
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:11,734
The fix is simple: try several starts, keep the best.

65
00:04:11,734 --> 00:04:16,248
Build a few random seeds, greedy, and grow from every node.

66
00:04:16,248 --> 00:04:21,526
Drop any split that breaks the size rule, then keep the smallest cut.

67
00:04:21,526 --> 00:04:27,494
Here, greedy and the five grows give three, five, five, nine, three and three.

68
00:04:27,494 --> 00:04:31,242
So the best is three: A, B and C against D and E.

69
00:04:31,242 --> 00:04:35,832
Pause and guess: can a smaller cut ever be the worse choice?

70
00:04:35,832 --> 00:04:39,492
Three traps can fool a method that looks sensible.

71
00:04:39,492 --> 00:04:42,932
And yes, a smaller cut can be the worse choice.

72
00:04:42,932 --> 00:04:47,251
Here is a star: A in the middle, wired to five other cells.

73
00:04:47,251 --> 00:04:52,301
An older greedy had no size limit, so it kept adding cells next to A.

74
00:04:52,301 --> 00:04:56,400
It ended with five cells against one, and a cut of five.

75
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:02,100
With six cells, each side must hold exactly three, so that split is not legal.

76
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:06,485
Our greedy stops each side at three cells, and gets fifteen.

77
00:05:06,485 --> 00:05:09,408
No legal split of this star does better.

78
00:05:09,408 --> 00:05:13,001
In this graph, A, B and D, E both weigh five.

79
00:05:13,001 --> 00:05:16,434
Greedy must pick one of them to start from.

80
00:05:16,434 --> 00:05:21,864
The rule picks the smaller name, A, B, and that gives a cut of nine.

81
00:05:21,864 --> 00:05:25,738
Starting from D, E instead gives a cut of four.

82
00:05:25,738 --> 00:05:29,036
So a tie rule more than doubled the cut.

83
00:05:29,036 --> 00:05:31,344
Grow from C also finds four.

84
00:05:31,344 --> 00:05:36,464
Here are two triangles, joined by one bridge, C, D, with weight four.

85
00:05:36,464 --> 00:05:38,912
Every triangle edge weighs three.

86
00:05:38,912 --> 00:05:43,736
Greedy starts from the heaviest edge, which is the bridge itself.

87
00:05:43,736 --> 00:05:48,336
So C and D begin on the same side, and the cut ends at twelve.

88
00:05:48,336 --> 00:05:53,506
Grow from A keeps its triangle together, and cuts only the bridge: four.

89
00:05:53,506 --> 00:05:57,168
Pause and guess: is grow always better than greedy?

90
00:05:57,168 --> 00:06:00,000
Now you get to build the splits yourself.

91
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,733
And no, grow is not always better: on our first graph, greedy found three, and grow

92
00:06:05,733 --> 00:06:06,907
from A only five.

93
00:06:06,907 --> 00:06:12,294
In the browser lab, you click cells to move them between sides, and watch both

94
00:06:12,294 --> 00:06:13,330
numbers change.

95
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:16,162
You can also run random, greedy and grow.

96
00:06:16,162 --> 00:06:20,928
The challenges check the split you built, not the button you clicked.

97
00:06:20,928 --> 00:06:25,135
If you like programming, write the three recipes in Python.

98
00:06:25,135 --> 00:06:29,058
The examples file gives the exact rules, ties included.

99
00:06:29,058 --> 00:06:32,694
Run the checker once to print every correct answer.

100
00:06:32,694 --> 00:06:34,762
Then run it on your own file.

101
00:06:34,762 --> 00:06:40,254
It also feeds the graph in reverse order, so a missing tie rule shows up as a

102
00:06:40,254 --> 00:06:40,824
failure.

103
00:06:40,824 --> 00:06:43,726
Random is fast, but it depends on luck.

104
00:06:43,726 --> 00:06:48,637
Greedy keeps heavy edges inside, but ties and bridges can fool it.

105
00:06:48,637 --> 00:06:50,943
Grow depends on its start node.

106
00:06:50,943 --> 00:06:56,226
Report the balance with every cut, and never let a side pass its limit.

107
00:06:56,226 --> 00:07:00,319
Break ties by name, not by the order of the input file.

108
00:07:00,319 --> 00:07:03,816
So try several starts, keep the best legal one.

109
00:07:03,816 --> 00:07:07,292
Pick a track: the browser lab, Python, or both.

110
00:07:07,292 --> 00:07:11,507
Tick each box of the checklist, then take the short quiz.

111
00:07:11,507 --> 00:07:17,128
Next comes Kernighan, Lin, which starts from your split and makes it better.

112
00:07:17,128 --> 00:07:22,083
Pause and guess: how much can it shrink the unlucky cut of sixteen?

113
00:07:22,083 --> 00:07:23,784
Lab four will show you.
